 "limitations": [
  "Labels are produced by an AI annotator (Claude) following the project's labelling guide, not by humans, and the gold set is annotated by the same family: shared bias is possible and is not measured. The annotator's self-consistency (training labels) bounds noise only. A human adjudication of the 60 queued gold rows is still outstanding.",
  "The abstention policy was changed once after a first full evaluation (from two tuned thresholds to one fixed commit threshold). The first run's numbers are kept in docs/evidence/ and discussed in docs/EVENT_MODEL.md; the gold and replay-check sets are therefore not perfectly untouched by design choices.",
  "Roughly 3,000 annotated rows. Rare classes (GEOPOLITICAL, CREDIT_FUNDING, ...) have few examples: see the per-class reliability table. A class flagged unreliable is never asserted; its documents are shown as UNCERTAIN.",
  "NONE (not a market event) is a judgement call (a scheduling notice, a promotion, an opinion without a fact). A different convention would move the NONE rate and the EARNINGS numbers.",
  "Dev rows select the model, fit its calibration and decide which classes are flagged unreliable. Scores on dev rows are therefore optimistic: use the gold and replay-check rows, which no choice depended on.",
  "Event type is judged from the headline or tweet alone (no article body, no price data). Multi-event texts get the dominant event only; there is no second label.",
  "Bag-of-words model: it does not understand negation scope, numbers or entities (it classifies the text, not the company).",
  "The model was fitted on train only and never refitted on dev, so it uses about 75% of the annotated rows.",
  "TFN crosswalk silver rows (if used) carry topic-derived, not event-derived, labels; the ablation in this card shows whether they helped.",
  "Probability calibration was fitted on dev rows; on other sources probabilities can be over-confident (see ECE per evaluation set)."
 ]
}
